<?php

namespace Database\Seeders;

use App\Models\KnowledgeBaseArticle;
use App\Models\KnowledgeBaseCategory;
use App\Models\User;
use Illuminate\Database\Seeder;
use Illuminate\Support\Carbon;

/**
 * PRODUCTION-SAFE knowledge base for AFFILIATES (audience 'affiliates') — quick
 * reference that complements the Academy: what the product does, how to sell it,
 * and how you get paid. Keyed by slug with updateOrCreate so re-running refreshes
 * the shipped content to the current version of the product.
 *
 *   php artisan db:seed --class=Database\\Seeders\\AffiliateKnowledgeBaseSeeder
 */
class AffiliateKnowledgeBaseSeeder extends Seeder
{
    public function run(): void
    {
        $authorId = User::where('role', USER_ROLE_ADMIN)->value('id') ?? User::value('id');
        if (! $authorId) {
            return;
        }

        $categories = [
            'af-getting-started' => ['Getting started', 'The opportunity, how you earn, and the Academy.', 'ri-rocket-line', 1],
            'af-the-product'     => ['What you’re selling', 'Understand Centresidence inside out.', 'ri-building-4-line', 2],
            'af-selling'         => ['Selling & the pipeline', 'Finding owners, demos, objections, closing.', 'ri-user-star-line', 3],
            'af-earnings'        => ['Earnings & your tools', 'Commissions, withdrawals, referrals, integrity.', 'ri-coins-line', 4],
        ];

        $cat = [];
        foreach ($categories as $slug => [$name, $desc, $icon, $order]) {
            $cat[$slug] = KnowledgeBaseCategory::updateOrCreate(['slug' => $slug], [
                'name' => $name, 'description' => $desc, 'icon' => $icon,
                'audience' => 'affiliates', 'sort_order' => $order, 'is_active' => true,
            ]);
        }

        foreach ($this->articles() as $i => $a) {
            KnowledgeBaseArticle::updateOrCreate(['slug' => $a['slug']], [
                'kb_category_id' => $cat[$a['category']]->id, 'created_by' => $authorId, 'updated_by' => $authorId,
                'title' => $a['title'], 'type' => 'article', 'audience' => 'affiliates',
                'status' => 'published', 'excerpt' => $a['excerpt'], 'body' => $a['body'],
                'sort_order' => $i + 1, 'published_at' => Carbon::now(),
            ]);
        }
    }

    private function articles(): array
    {
        return [
            // ── Getting started ────────────────────────────────────────────
            [
                'slug' => 'af-welcome', 'category' => 'af-getting-started',
                'title' => 'Welcome — the opportunity and how you earn',
                'excerpt' => 'You introduce property owners to Centresidence and earn a first-time plus recurring commission on each one.',
                'body' => '<h3>Your role</h3><p>Centresidence is an all-in-one platform for property owners — automatic M-Pesa rent collection, tenant/unit management, invoicing, maintenance, reporting and even infrastructure financing repaid from rent. Your job is to <strong>introduce owners and help them get started</strong>; the platform is built to be plug-and-play, so once they’re in, the system does the heavy lifting.</p><h3>How you earn</h3><ul><li><strong>First-time commission</strong> — when a referred owner converts to a paying subscription.</li><li><strong>Recurring commission</strong> — a share of their ongoing payments for as long as the arrangement runs, so a growing base of active owners means growing passive income.</li></ul><p>Rates are set by Centresidence and shown in your dashboard. Earnings accrue to a balance you withdraw to M-Pesa.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-the-academy', 'category' => 'af-getting-started',
                'title' => 'The Academy — why it’s required',
                'excerpt' => 'Pass the training (80% per module) to unlock your affiliate tools. It teaches you the whole product.',
                'body' => '<h3>Complete it first</h3><p>Your tools — Leads, Referrals, Marketing materials, Commissions and Withdrawals — unlock only after you finish the <strong>Academy</strong>. Each module ends with a short quiz; you need <strong>80%</strong> to pass, with up to 3 attempts before a module is flagged for review.</p><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>To sell Centresidence well you have to understand it. The Academy walks you through the entire product and the craft of selling it, so you can talk to any landlord with confidence. Treat it as your playbook — and come back to this Knowledge Base whenever you need a quick refresher.</p>',
            ],

            // ── What you’re selling ────────────────────────────────────────
            [
                'slug' => 'af-product-rent', 'category' => 'af-the-product',
                'title' => 'The core — properties, tenants & rent collection',
                'excerpt' => 'Owners manage buildings and units, onboard tenants, and collect rent automatically over M-Pesa.',
                'body' => '<h3>Properties & units</h3><p>Owners set up buildings as <strong>properties</strong> split into <strong>units</strong>, each with its own rent, deposit and late-fee rules — a clean map of everything they own.</p><h3>Tenants</h3><p>Owners add tenants to units, accept <strong>tenant applications</strong>, and keep full <strong>tenant history</strong>. Each tenant gets a secure login to view invoices, pay, raise issues and read notices.</p><h3>Rent collection — the headline</h3><p>Centresidence auto-generates each rent invoice and the tenant pays via an <strong>M-Pesa prompt on their phone</strong>; the owner sees the payment and receipt <em>instantly</em>. <strong>Recurring rent</strong> regenerates invoices every cycle automatically. The whole <strong>Billing Center</strong> handles deposits, utilities, incidentals and late fees the same easy way. This is the feature that sells itself — most landlords’ biggest pain is chasing rent.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-product-toolkit', 'category' => 'af-the-product',
                'title' => 'The full management toolkit',
                'excerpt' => 'Maintenance, tickets, notices, expenses, reports, documents, e-sign agreements, listings, SMS and screening.',
                'body' => '<h3>One system for everything</h3><ul><li><strong>Maintenance & maintainers</strong> — tenants log requests; owners assign a caretaker and track to completion.</li><li><strong>Tickets</strong> — a tenant↔owner support channel.</li><li><strong>Notice Board</strong> — broadcast notices to a unit or whole property.</li><li><strong>Expenses & Reports</strong> — record costs and see earnings, profit/loss, <strong>occupancy</strong>, lease and maintenance figures.</li><li><strong>Documents</strong> — store leases, IDs and files securely.</li><li><strong>Agreements (e-sign)</strong> — send a lease to sign in-app, verified by an SMS one-time code, producing a certified PDF and audit trail.</li><li><strong>Listings</strong> — advertise vacant units to cut void periods.</li><li><strong>SMS credits</strong> — message tenants directly.</li><li><strong>Tenant screening</strong> — check a prospective tenant before letting.</li></ul><p><em>Pitch:</em> replace a drawer of receipts, a noisy WhatsApp group and a rent notebook with one organised system. Show the two or three that hit the owner’s pain.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-product-money-engine', 'category' => 'af-the-product',
                'title' => 'The money engine — pricing, marketplace & financing',
                'excerpt' => 'Transaction pricing (pay-as-you-go), a tenant marketplace, and infrastructure financing repaid from rent.',
                'body' => '<h3>Start on Free — the "get them in" plan</h3><p>Every owner can start on the <strong>Free</strong> plan: it is <strong>permanent</strong> (no subscription fee), manages <strong>1–{{free_cap}} units</strong>, and the system is fully usable on it — they collect rent, manage tenants and units, and only pay for what they actually use (SMS, screening, agreements, marketplace). This is your zero-barrier opener: "nothing to lose, set up one property and see it working." A Free owner still earns you money through usage, and they upgrade to a paid plan later because paid plans make that usage <em>cheaper</em> — not because anything was locked away.</p><h3>Why owners upgrade — the numbers, laid bare</h3><p><strong>This is your upgrade pitch, and these figures are live from our current pricing — quote them with confidence.</strong> On Free, every usage line is charged at full rate; on a paid plan those same lines get cheaper or become included. So the more tenants an owner runs, the more a paid plan saves them:</p><ul><li><strong>Tenant screening</strong> — Free: {{screening_free_quota}} free checks a month, then {{screening_price}} per check. Paid plans: <strong>unlimited, included</strong>.</li><li><strong>E-sign agreements</strong> — Free: {{agreement_free_quota}} free a month, then {{agreement_price}} per agreement. Paid plans: <strong>unlimited, included</strong>.</li><li><strong>SMS to tenants</strong> — Free: {{sms_free_price}} per SMS. Paid plans: {{sms_paid_price}} per SMS (roughly 3× cheaper).</li><li><strong>Marketplace commission</strong> — Free owners pay the highest platform cut on marketplace sales; each paid tier pays progressively less.</li></ul><p><strong>So the pitch is never "unlock features" — it\'s "you\'re already paying per screening, per agreement and per SMS; on a paid plan screening and agreements stop costing you, and your SMS gets about 3× cheaper. The bigger you grow, the more it saves you."</strong> Watch an owner\'s usage — when they\'re regularly buying credits, that\'s the moment to walk them through the maths.</p><h3>Flexible pricing</h3><p>Owners choose a <strong>subscription</strong> or <strong>transaction (pay-as-you-go)</strong> mode, where the platform takes a small percentage of rent collected — no monthly fee. "You only pay when you collect" wins hesitant owners.</p><h3>Tenant marketplace (My Shop)</h3><p>Owners can sell products and services to their own tenants (water, gas, cleaning, and more) via M-Pesa — an extra income stream inside the building.</p><h3>Infrastructure financing — the standout</h3><p>Owners install <strong>smart infrastructure</strong> (prepaid meters, smart locks) financed by a partner and <strong>repaid automatically from rent at source</strong>. Tenants top up utilities by M-Pesa; that usage becomes prepaid, recurring income; and the owner upgrades their property with <em>no upfront cash</em>. When an owner says "I already have a system", these differentiators are your answer.</p>',
            ],

            // ── Selling & the pipeline ─────────────────────────────────────
            [
                'slug' => 'af-finding-leads', 'category' => 'af-selling',
                'title' => 'Finding & qualifying leads',
                'excerpt' => 'Target multi-unit landlords, qualify fast, and register every prospect to protect it for 60 days.',
                'body' => '<h3>Who to target</h3><p>Landlords with several rental units, small agencies, and owners still chasing rent by hand. More units = more value to them and more recurring commission to you.</p><h3>Where to find them</h3><p>Warm referrals, landlord and estate groups, caretakers who know owners, property managers, and simply asking who owns a building. A qualified warm lead beats a big cold list.</p><h3>★ New buildings & developments — the best channel</h3><p>Newly built and still-under-construction residential buildings are your strongest source: the owner hasn’t chosen how they’ll run the place yet, so there’s no system to migrate off and no habit to break. Walk the new developments going up in your area, reach the owner, developer or site manager <strong>before tenants move in</strong>, and get the building onto Centresidence from day one. If you’ve worked in construction you already know these owners — a warm introduction beats any cold call. There’s a full playbook for this in <strong>“Prospecting: new construction sites.”</strong></p><h3>Qualify fast</h3><p>Learn: how many units? how do they collect rent today? what frustrates them most? That tells you the fit and which feature to lead with.</p><h3>Capture & protect</h3><p>Register every prospect under <strong>Leads</strong> immediately. It organises your pipeline and <strong>protects the lead as yours for 60 days</strong> — an unregistered lead can’t be credited to you. Capture first, sell second.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-construction-sites', 'category' => 'af-selling',
                'title' => 'Prospecting: new construction sites',
                'excerpt' => 'New residential buildings are future clients — reach the owner before tenants move in. Contractors have a warm-intro edge.',
                'body' => '<h3>Why new sites are gold</h3><p>Every new residential building is a future client, and the best time to reach the owner is <strong>before tenants move in</strong>, while they’re still deciding how they’ll run the place. Walk the developments coming up in your area and you’ll meet owners and developers who don’t yet have a system.</p><h3>How to work a site</h3><ul><li><strong>Target sites at the finishing stage</strong> — handover is close and the owner is thinking about rent collection, units and tenants.</li><li><strong>Ask for the developer, owner, or site/project manager</strong> — they’ll point you to whoever decides.</li><li><strong>Lead with what you solve</strong> — collect rent, track units, screen tenants, and get paid on time from the very first tenant, free to start.</li><li><strong>Offer a guided demo on the spot.</strong> A building that opens on Centresidence from day one never has to migrate later.</li></ul><h3>Contractors have an edge</h3><p>If you’ve worked in construction, you already know the owners of projects you’ve delivered. A warm introduction — <em>“the system I’d use to run this building”</em> — beats any cold call. You’re introducing them to Centresidence; they choose to adopt it, but your relationship opens the door.</p><h3>Filling the empty units</h3><p>A brand-new building also has many empty units to fill, and that’s where <strong>House Hunt</strong>, our tenant network, comes in. Be honest that it’s still growing — a <em>rising asset</em> rather than an instant fix: the more owners and tenants join, the better it connects vacant units with people looking for a home. Offer it as a reason to get on board <strong>now</strong> and grow with it, not as a promise of instant tenants.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-running-a-demo', 'category' => 'af-selling',
                'title' => 'Running a great demo',
                'excerpt' => 'Ask about their pain, show M-Pesa rent collection, layer 2–3 relevant features, then close to a trial.',
                'body' => '<h3>A winning structure</h3><ol><li><strong>Ask first</strong> — how do they collect rent today, and what’s most painful?</li><li><strong>Show the fix</strong> — invoice → tenant M-Pesa prompt → owner sees payment + receipt instantly. This one flow wins most demos.</li><li><strong>Layer 2–3 relevant features</strong> — tenants/units, maintenance, notices, reports/occupancy or e-sign agreements — only what matches their pain.</li><li><strong>Show a differentiator</strong> — transaction pricing, the marketplace, or rent-repaid infrastructure.</li><li><strong>Close</strong> — get them <strong>live now</strong>: set them up on <strong>Free</strong> on the call (fastest, zero barrier). Offer a <strong>trial</strong> only if this particular owner wants to feel the full paid experience first — it is a tool for the lead who needs that nudge, not the automatic close for everyone.</li></ol><p><strong>Do</strong> keep it about them and go slow on the one flow that matters. <strong>Don’t</strong> tour every menu, dwell on price, or over-promise. Update the lead’s stage as you go.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-objections', 'category' => 'af-selling',
                'title' => 'Handling common objections',
                'excerpt' => 'Answers to "too expensive", "I have a system", "tenants won’t use M-Pesa", and "let me think".',
                'body' => '<ul><li><strong>"Too expensive."</strong> → Transaction mode: no monthly fee, just a small % of rent you collect; it pays for itself the first time it saves a missed rent.</li><li><strong>"I already have a system / a book."</strong> → Ask what it does when a tenant is late or when they need last year’s numbers. Show automatic M-Pesa collection, occupancy/reports, and the marketplace + rent-repaid infrastructure a notebook can’t match.</li><li><strong>"My tenants won’t use M-Pesa."</strong> → They already pay by M-Pesa; here it’s just a prompt + a receipt, easier than sending manually.</li><li><strong>"I don’t trust money online."</strong> → Rent settles to the owner, every payment has a receipt, access is secure — transparency is the point.</li><li><strong>"Let me think about it."</strong> → Offer a no-risk trial on one property.</li></ul><p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> never invent an answer. If unsure, say you’ll find out and check this Knowledge Base — honesty closes more than bluffing.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-pipeline', 'category' => 'af-selling',
                'title' => 'The pipeline: lead → demo → account (trial optional)',
                'excerpt' => 'Move each lead through the stages, follow up promptly, and work inside the 60-day window. The trial is optional — not every lead needs one.',
                'body' => '<h3>The stages</h3><ol><li><strong>Lead</strong> — captured.</li><li><strong>Demo</strong> — shown the product.</li><li><strong>Trial (optional)</strong> — a time-boxed taste of the full <em>paid</em> experience. See the note below — it is NOT an automatic step.</li><li><strong>Account</strong> — they get a live account (usually on <strong>Free</strong>) and start collecting rent; your commission grows with their activity. You do this with the <strong>“Set up on Free”</strong> action on the lead (see <em>“Helping your owner go live”</em>): it invites the owner, they activate by setting their own password, and the lead becomes <strong>converted</strong>, attributed to you.</li></ol><h3>★ The trial is a tool, not a step — request it only when the lead wants it</h3><p>Because every owner can start on <strong>Free</strong> straight away — permanent, fully usable, nothing to lose — a trial is <strong>not</strong> something every lead needs. Many owners are perfectly happy to begin on Free and simply start collecting rent. <strong>Request a trial only when a specific lead wants to try the full paid experience first</strong> (e.g. they want to feel unlimited screening/agreements or the paid features before deciding). Treating "request trial" as an automatic next step after a demo is the wrong instinct: for most leads, the stronger move is to get them <strong>live on Free today</strong> and let the system prove itself. A trial is your closing tool for the lead who specifically needs that nudge — not a box to tick on everyone.</p><h3>Work it</h3><p>Update each lead’s stage as it moves so you know where to spend time and are credited correctly. <strong>Follow up promptly</strong> — most deals are lost to silence, not a "no". A check-in after the demo (and again after a trial starts, if you granted one) wins conversions. Keep momentum inside the <strong>60-day</strong> ownership window.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-onboard-your-owner', 'category' => 'af-selling',
                'title' => 'Helping your owner go live',
                'excerpt' => 'Signing them up is step one — getting them collecting rent is what pays you. A simple first-session checklist.',
                'body' => '<h3>Why this is your job — and your income</h3><p>Getting an owner <em>signed up</em> is only half the win. You earn as your owners <strong>use</strong> the system — rent collected, tenants screened, agreements signed. An owner who signs up and then sits idle earns you nothing and is likely to drift away. So the most valuable thing you do is stay on the call (or a follow-up) and get them to their <strong>first real rent collection</strong>. Once they feel that, they stay — and your recurring income starts to compound.</p><h3>The first-session checklist (about 15 minutes)</h3><ol><li><strong>Add their first property.</strong> From the owner dashboard → <em>Properties</em> → add the building, then split it into <strong>units</strong> with each unit’s rent, deposit and any late-fee rule. Start with one real property — they can repeat the pattern for the rest.</li><li><strong>Add a tenant to a unit.</strong> Enter one current tenant so the owner sees the system working on someone real. The tenant gets their own secure login for invoices and payments.</li><li><strong>Generate the first invoice and collect.</strong> Let them watch a rent invoice go out and get paid by the <strong>M-Pesa prompt</strong> on the tenant’s phone — the receipt lands instantly. This is the moment that sells itself; don’t skip it.</li><li><strong>Hand them the on-site QR.</strong> See below — the fastest way to onboard the rest of their tenants without you or them typing each one in.</li></ol><h3>The tenant-onboarding QR card — your best friend</h3><p>Centresidence generates a <strong>printable per-property onboarding card</strong>: the owner opens a property and prints a card carrying a <strong>QR code</strong>. They pin it on the building’s notice board or gate. Any tenant scans it with their phone and is taken straight to the <strong>get-started</strong> on-ramp to install the app and sign in — no manual, per-tenant training needed. Show every owner this: it turns onboarding a whole building into "print one page, stick it on the wall."</p><h3>★ Set them up on Free — one button, from the lead</h3><p>You don’t wait for admin and you don’t create the account yourself. Open the lead and use <strong>“Set up on Free”</strong> on the Sales Actions card — it’s the primary, default action there. That sends the owner an invite to their email (and SMS); they <strong>activate the account themselves by choosing a password</strong>, which means the account is created on <em>their</em> consent. You’ll see the lead switch to <em>“Free invite sent — awaiting activation”</em>, and once they accept, it flips to <strong>converted</strong> and is attributed to you automatically. Nothing exists until they accept, and the invite lapses after a week if they don’t — so <strong>follow up and nudge them to activate</strong>; that’s on you. A demo first is recommended, but if a lead is ready (a warm referral, a new-building owner who already said yes), you can set them up on Free straight away — you don’t have to run a demo first.</p><h3>Start free, stay honest</h3><p>Free is the plan they land on (1–{{free_cap}} units, permanent, fully usable). There’s no reason to wait — they collect rent today and only pay for what they use. They’ll move to a paid plan later because it makes that usage cheaper, not because you pushed it. Be the partner who got them <em>working</em>, not just signed up.</p><h3>After the first session</h3><p>Check in once their first rent lands, and again a week later. If anything is unclear, they can reach Centresidence support in-app (Support) at any time — you’re their first line, we’re the backstop. <em>(Coming soon: scan-to-pay QR tabs so tenants can pay at the property by scanning — we’ll update this article when it lands.)</em></p>',
            ],

            // ── Earnings & your tools ──────────────────────────────────────
            [
                'slug' => 'af-commissions', 'category' => 'af-earnings',
                'title' => 'Commissions — how you’re paid',
                'excerpt' => 'First-time + recurring across everything your owners do — subscriptions, rent, marketplace, screening, agreements and financing — not just their plan.',
                'body' => '<h3>Two types, on every line</h3><p>You earn two ways: a <strong>first-time commission</strong> the first time an owner does something, and a <strong>recurring commission</strong> — a share for the months that follow. What changed is the <em>scope</em>: this now applies across <strong>everything your owners do on the platform</strong>, not just their plan fee:</p><ul><li><strong>Subscriptions</strong> — when a referred owner converts to a paying plan, and on their ongoing payments.</li><li><strong>Rent</strong> — a small share of the rent your owners collect through the platform.</li><li><strong>Marketplace</strong> — a cut of our commission when owners sell to their tenants.</li><li><strong>Tenant screening</strong> — when an owner runs a paid tenant check (small per use).</li><li><strong>Agreements</strong> — when an owner sends a paid e-sign lease (small per use).</li><li><strong>★ Financing — your biggest single pay-out.</strong> When an owner you introduced takes <strong>infrastructure financing</strong>, you earn the first-time share of <em>our origination fee</em> on the facility, then the recurring share on any further facilities within the window. Because a facility is a large sum, that one-time cut can <strong>out-earn months of subscription commission</strong> — a single financing deal is one of the most valuable things you can land. See <em>“Finance products in depth”</em> in the academy for how to spot and open these conversations.</li></ul><h3>Where to put your energy</h3><p>Not all lines are equal. Rank them by what they actually pay you: <strong>financing</strong> (big one-off per facility) → <strong>Transaction-mode rent</strong> (steady monthly on real rent) → <strong>subscription upgrades</strong> (recurring) → marketplace → screening/agreements (small). So the highest-leverage moves are: land <strong>financing</strong> deals, move active rent-collectors onto <strong>Transaction mode</strong>, and upgrade growing owners to a <strong>paid plan</strong>. For the full breakdown — every channel ranked, the honest picture, and what a realistic book of owners pays — see <em>“How much you can earn — your channels, ranked.”</em></p><h3>Why this matters to you</h3><p>Even an owner on the <strong>free</strong> plan earns you money every time they screen a tenant, send an agreement, or take financing. You’re paid for the <strong>activity you bring</strong>, not just the plan they sit on — so help your owners get value and stay active. Your dashboard shows <em>“what your owners generated for you this month”</em>, broken down by line.</p><h3>Your balance</h3><p>All of it accrues to your balance under <strong>Commissions</strong>, where you can see each event and its status and withdraw to M-Pesa. Rates are configured by Centresidence and shown in your dashboard.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-earning-channels', 'category' => 'af-earnings',
                'title' => 'How much you can earn — your channels, ranked',
                'excerpt' => 'The honest, full picture of every way you earn, ranked by value — and what a realistic book of owners pays. Partner-confidential.',
                'body' => '<div style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;background:#FEF3E7;border:1px solid #F3DBB3;border-radius:999px;padding:5px 13px;margin-bottom:16px;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#854F0B;">🔒 Confidential</div><h3>First, the honest truth</h3><p>This is a <strong>business you build</strong>, not passive money. You earn as the owners you bring on <em>use</em> the platform, and each earning line runs for <strong>{{recurring_months}} months</strong> per owner — so steady income comes from steadily bringing on new owners <em>and</em> helping the ones you have do more. Done consistently it can grow into a full-time income; treated as “sign up and forget,” it won’t. We’d rather tell you that plainly than over-promise.</p><h3>Your earning lines, ranked by what they actually pay</h3><p>Not all lines are equal. Put your energy where the money is:</p><ol><li><strong>★ Financing — your biggest single pay-out.</strong> When an owner takes <strong>infrastructure financing</strong>, you earn <strong>{{first_rate}}</strong> of our origination fee on the facility (then <strong>{{recurring_rate}}</strong> on any further facilities within the window). A facility is a large sum, so <strong>one deal can out-earn months of subscription commission</strong>. If your network reaches owners doing utility/meter upgrades — or people in banking/credit — that’s your highest-value lane.</li><li><strong>Transaction-mode rent.</strong> When an owner is on <strong>Transaction mode</strong>, you earn <strong>{{rent_commission}}</strong> of all the rent they collect, every month for {{recurring_months}} months. Steady, and it scales with their portfolio — moving active rent-collectors onto Transaction is the best recurring move you can make.</li><li><strong>Subscription upgrades.</strong> When an owner moves to a paid plan, you earn <strong>{{first_rate}}</strong> of the first payment, then <strong>{{recurring_rate}}</strong> each month for {{recurring_months}} months.</li><li><strong>Marketplace.</strong> Up to <strong>{{marketplace_cut}}</strong> of our commission on what owners sell their tenants — biggest on Free owners.</li><li><strong>Screening &amp; agreements.</strong> The same first-time-then-recurring share of our small fee on each use — modest, but they add up across a big book, and they mean <em>even Free owners earn you something</em>.</li></ol><h3>What a realistic book looks like</h3><p>Your income is the sum of many owners’ activity, each inside their {{recurring_months}}-month window. A few busy Transaction owners, a financing deal or two, and a growing base of paid-plan owners compound faster than you’d expect. Open the <strong>earnings calculator</strong> on your Academy home page and move the sliders — every figure is pre-set to our exact live rates, so what you see is what you’d earn.</p><h3>The one rule</h3><p>Because each line runs for {{recurring_months}} months, your income holds and grows only if you keep the top of the funnel full: bring on new owners, push the high-value lines (financing, Transaction, upgrades), and help every owner do more. That’s the whole game.</p>',
            ],
            [
                'slug' => 'af-withdrawals', 'category' => 'af-earnings',
